## Problem

The third time I wrote a config loader that validates required environment
variables and throws a useful error, the pattern was obviously settled and
obviously in the wrong place. The same was true of the logger, the Mongo
connection helper, the DI container, the CORS-and-error-handler Hono setup, and
the token-refresh hook on the front end. Copy-paste means a fix lands in one repo
and rots in two.

## Approach

One repository, several small published packages, each with a narrow remit and no
ambition to be a framework. If a piece of code is genuinely the same in two apps,
it moves here; if it merely looks similar, it stays where it is.

## Architecture

A Bun workspace driven by Turborepo, built with tsup, published to npm.

- **`@imapps/api-utils`** — the server foundation. `createApp` returns a Hono app
  with an origin-reflecting CORS allow-list, request logging and a shared error
  handler already attached; alongside it sit the config service, structured
  logger, DI container, Mongo connection and MinIO object-store connection. It
  ships a `./hono` subpath export so a consumer that only wants the app factory
  doesn't pull in the database code.
- **`@imapps/web-utils`** — the client half of authentication: a
  `ProtectedRoute`, an auth context, and the `useTokenRefresh` /
  `useTokenInitialization` / `useAuthRedirect` hooks that make a `401` a
  transparent retry rather than a logout.
- **`@imapps/biome-config`** — one lint/format baseline, published as a single
  `base` preset, so "the rules" are a dependency bump for the repos that extend it.
- **`@imapps/dev-scripts`** — the `fallow` dead-code audit wired to run locally
  exactly as CI runs it, downloading the binary on first use.
- **`@imapps/kanban-cli`** — a CLI plus agent skill that drives a Kanban-board
  markdown file to completion: implement an item, run Playwright e2e as a merge
  gate, open a PR, wait for CI, merge, wait for the deploy, smoke-check live, and
  revert if that fails.

The repo also carries the shared CI layer the app repos consume as a submodule:
`pipelines/scripts` builds and publishes each service's Docker images from a
per-service JSON config, so adding an image to a service is a config entry rather
than another copy of a build script.

## Decisions and trade-offs

**One version across every package, stamped by semantic-release.** Independent
per-package versioning is the textbook answer for a monorepo and is genuinely
better when packages have independent consumers. These do not — they are consumed
together by the same four apps. A `stamp-versions` script writes the computed
release version into the root manifest and every workspace package, so
`@imapps/api-utils` and `@imapps/web-utils` are always the same commit — as I
write this, both sit at `0.11.0` on npm, as does `@imapps/kanban-cli`.
The cost is version churn in packages that did not change; the win is that
"which versions go together" is never a question.

**Subpath exports instead of one barrel.** `@imapps/api-utils` exports `.` and
`./hono` separately. A single entry point would be simpler to publish and would
mean every consumer of `createApp` also resolves the MongoDB and MinIO clients.
kivo is small on purpose, and that mattered.

**Extract after the copy, not before it.** Nothing here was designed as a shared
package first. The auth hooks and `ProtectedRoute` lived in shoppingo and were
lifted into `web-utils` only once jewellery-catalogue needed the same behaviour;
shoppingo's own auth components were deleted in the same pass. That ordering is
why the packages are small and why nothing in them is configurable "just in
case" — the second call site defines the interface, not a guess about a third.

## Outcome

Consumed by shoppingo, jewellery-catalogue, kivo and mixtape. CI is lint → test →
semantic-release → publish changed packages to npm, all on Bun.

The README is the weakest part and says so implicitly — it still documents a
Yarn/GitLab publishing flow that has been GitHub Actions and Bun for some time.
The `templates.yml` GitLab CI template is likewise marked deprecated in place
rather than deleted; that is a cleanup I owe the repo.
